WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
On the Home screen, click Open project… and choose the project of the second tutorial.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The project opens up to date: nothing is to be computed.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Click Post.

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Post shows the image of the second tutorial: clean and quiet, but tightly cropped. Before any gesture, look at what the crop left out.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Click Crop: the whole field appears around the frame.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.000
At the left edge, outside the frame, a dark stain: the shadow of a dust grain. The Spot healing tool will repair it.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.000
In the corners and along the edges, the stars are comet-shaped: the coma of the telescope. The Synthetic stars block will redraw them.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.000
What is right stays as it is: the dark, neutral sky, the smooth gas and the star settings of the second tutorial.

9
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Click Graph.

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
In the palette, double-click Synthetic stars: the block joins the workflow.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The block sits after Stars/background, with the method Image shape: the stars are redrawn with the shape measured at the centre of the field, where it is round.

12
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Click Run: only this block is computed.

13
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
The list shows every block up to date, except Synthetic stars.

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Not shown in real time: the run took 11 s.

15
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Click Post.

16
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:17.000
The Stars group now holds two rows: Synthetic stars, with a white mask, and Real stars. White means that the synthetic stars replace the real ones everywhere.

17
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Click Crop.

18
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Drag the top left corner of the frame to the corner of the field.

19
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Drag the bottom right corner to the edge as well, keeping the black borders of the alignment out.

20
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Click Apply to keep the crop.

21
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.000
The whole field is back, with the stain and the comet-shaped stars.

22
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Click the Starless image row.

23
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Click Spot healing: its filter is added to the starless image.

24
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Drag Size to the right until the circle covers the whole stain with a margin, here about 140 px.

25
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Click on the stain: it is replaced by sky taken beside it.

26
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Reduce Size a little for the small stain beside it, here about 90 px.

27
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Click on the small stain.

28
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
The sky is even where the stain was, with no lighter or darker patch where the sky was taken.

29
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Click the Synthetic stars row.

30
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Click its mask: the brush appears, set to Subtract.

31
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Drag Size to the right until the brush is as large as it goes.

32
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Drag Hardness to the left, to 0: the edge of the stroke fades out.

33
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Paint one stroke across the centre of the field: the real stars come back there.

34
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.000
The red tint shows where the mask hides the synthetic stars: the centre, with a soft edge.

35
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Nothing shows where the mask fades, and the density of the stars does not jump.

36
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.000
In the corner, the comet-shaped stars are now round; the brightest ones are real.

37
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.000
In the centre, the real stars are kept, with their spikes.

38
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Click Export…

39
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Choose JPEG in Format.

40
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Click Export.

41
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
The image is written, with its processing history beside it.

42
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
The image is exported. Press Ctrl+S to keep the project.
